ilitants have so far  failed and many fear the violence will  only get worse  once the last u.s soldiers have returned  home  franz marty is a freelance journalist in  afghanistan he joins me now from the  capital kabul  france welcome the us troops leaving the  country and of course those  recent mainly rural gains made by the  taliban how likely  do you think it is that the entire  country could fall to the taliban  president biden timed this term this  scenario highly unlikely and i  as well as other knowledgeable analysts  agree this is not to say that the  situation  is not very dire as you said they have  made  unprecedented gains 150 plus districts  in the past a little bit over two months  that they have taken over however it is  noteworthy that so far they haven't  made a serious attempt to take over  provincial capital  this seems from the taliban side to be a  deliberate decision  um because they seem to now have the  strategy to take over rural areas  and um demand a surrender of the afghan  government  at the negotiating table uh and they  will pursue that  in the like foreseeable future as far as  it looks now  uh even if they would abandon this  strategy and would try  uh the advances have been so fast uh  there is a question whether the taliban  have enough enough manpower  to like actually take over everything  they might be able to take over several  provinces but  not uh swiftly the whole country um  in fact i have spoken by telephone to  people in like  newly conquered district and they told  me that barely any taliban are left  because they moved them  to the new front lines so that's why  fall of the whole country is still  unlikely at this time  right france after 20 years of  international support the afghan  national army is still not able to  completely defeat the taliban and  it seems not even really keep them at  bay can you explain why that is  uh there are several reasons and one  thing that is important to know  the us presence was already before  president biden announced a withdrawal  pretty small and they didn't do that  much so most operations were done  by afghan government forces so now that  they  uh lost that many districts seems to be  more uh psychological issue in many  districts  government forces abandoned them uh  without being like really forced by the  taliban it wasn't that they the taliban  have this overwhelming military power to  really force them out  um and then yeah several districts were  abandoned like that without apparently  uh due to a clear plan  um another thing is that like the  taliban as a geria movement  they have like an advantage in this  asymmetric warfare and  at the end reality is also in not in  significant parts of the population the  taliban have support  especially in rural areas so uh there it  is sometimes that the taliban have more  support than the government and that  allows them to to like make them the  advances  and makes it difficult for the afghan  government forces okay friends we're  running out of time but i really wanted  to get a sense  about the afghan people themselves i  mean are they happy to see the end of  foreign troops in their country or  is there a sense they're being let down  by the us and its allies  it's very split some people profited uh  from  from the u.s presence uh from the  progress that was made  they are worried they fear what will  happen um  if the americans leave completely and if  the taliban  move forward like this there are others  that have seen little  benefit of the progress uh that remain  like uh  very rural very uh traditionalist  and religious and they are uh happy that  that like  troops depart so it's like really it's  uh you have everything  right journalist franz marti in kabul  afghanistan thanks very much for the  analysis  you
 

